    "q-336508": {
      "dom_id": "step-q336508",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "A wastewater treatment tank at a chemical plant can hold up to $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of wastewater with $40\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved contaminant. A more concentrated wastewater stream with concentration $1.25\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow.",
      "worked_solution": "Since $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.25\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of contaminant enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.25\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.25\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =15\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid; solution enters at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=120+12t-8t=4(30+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(8\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =15-\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q=15.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(30+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q) & =(30+t)^{2}\\cdot 15 \\\\ (30+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(30+t)Q & =15(30+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2}) & =15(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 15(30+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30+t)^{2} & =15\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(30+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =5(30+t)+\\frac{C}{(30+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $40\\,\\text{lb}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=40,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =40 \\\\ 5(30+0)+\\frac{C}{(30+0)^{2}} & =40 \\\\ 150+\\frac{C}{(30)^{2}} & =40 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{30}^{2}} & =-110 \\\\ C & =-110(30)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =5(30+t)-\\frac{110(30)^{2}}{(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =5(30+t)-110{(\\frac{30}{30+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $180\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=180$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =180 \\\\ 120+12{t}_{o}-8{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 120+4{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 4{t}_{o} & =60 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =15\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(15) & =5(30+15)-110{(\\frac{30}{30+15})}^{2} \\\\  & =225-110{(\\frac{30}{45})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈176.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
